<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medina Elvira</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Madinat Ilbira, Ilbira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> Elvira (Madinat Ilbira) was a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> near modern Atarfe, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, and the capital of the Elvira district under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. It grew near Roman Iliberri, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elvira">Council of Elvira</a> met in the early 4th century. In the civil wars after 1009 its people moved, under the Zirid leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawi-ibn-ziri">Zawi ibn Ziri</a>, to the more defensible hill of Granada, which replaced it as the regional centre.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under al-Mundhir (886–888) and Abd Allah (888–912), Arab lords in Seville and <b>Elvira</b> also rebelled, and for a time the emirs controlled little more than Córdoba and its surroundings, though Abd Allah defeated Ibn Hafsun at Poley in 891.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1013 the Sanhaja Berber leader Zawi ibn Ziri, a veteran of the caliphal wars, took over the district of <b>Elvira</b> and moved its centre to the more defensible hill of Granada.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-granada">Taifa of Granada</a></li></ul></section>
